Topi Linkala wrote:
> 
> OO is object orientation, in contrast to functionalism.

And also in contrast to procedural.

> It is a mental 
> design principle that doesn't need strict coding language nomore than 
> functionalism does.

That's correct. Though it's helpful to have some "engine" to do your
practise; C++ can be a choice here.

> What I meant is that C++ is not very good programming language to use 
> describing OO principles as it has many functional features (from C),

(ITYM procedural. C++'s STL has implemented some functional concepts.)

C++ is very bloated and you have much syntax ballast that may hide the
OO essential. Anyway, it supports an acceptable syntax to describe OO
concepts. What would you suggest to use instead? Use just UML diagrams
on paper? (Personally I'm no friend of learning just from diagrams.[*])

> but if you know OO principles C++ is very powerfull programming language 
> to use. Neither can I see any reason to learn C++ if one wants to learn C.

As C++ is quite a superset von C I suppose it may make some sense as
long as one is aware about where the differences and commonalities are.
But I agree that the personal choice should consider the application
context where one intends to work.

Janis

[*] I say that from the perspective of having "learned" OO from Simula
(at times where the term "OO" has not been a used, at least not widely),
